 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others to `t1..tN`
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: after a close the remaining tabs are renumbered `t1, t2, …` in tab order, so keys never have gaps, and
+  `next` is reset to `N + 1` (N = tabs left), so the next `openTab` gets `t{N+1}` and cannot collide. A key held outside
+  the state (the active tab) is carried across the renumber with `remapKey(state, closedKey, key)`, which returns the
+  tab's new key or `null` if that tab was the one closed.
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys (2026-09-02). Overridden by the maintainer on 2026-10-23 as an explicit, informed
+  override of that record; no new incident or measurement is claimed.
+- Reason: the maintainer wants keys, and the `?tabs=` URL state built from them, to stay contiguous after a close.
+- Consequences: accepted tradeoff, not mitigated by this code. The key is still the React key, so every tab to the
+  right of a closed one gets a new key and is remounted, losing its scroll position and unsaved form input (the
+  2026-08-29 incident). Closing the last tab renumbers nothing and is unaffected. Keeping that state across a close
+  now needs the UI layer to key components on something other than `tab.key`; this module has no stable id for it.
+  Anything else holding a key across a close must remap it with `remapKey` or it will point at a different tab: the
+  active-tab key, maps or caches keyed by tab key, and URLs saved before the close (`?tabs=t1,t3` is not a valid
+  state any more). None of these exist in this repository; they live in the consuming app and were not checked.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` (close first / middle / last, `remapKey` keeps the active tab on the same tab, open
+  after close). The remount itself cannot be tested here: no React in this repository.
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — SUPERSEDED on 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys: closing a tab never renumbers the others
+- Status: superseded. The maintainer overrode it on 2026-10-23. Kept for history; do not treat as in force.
 - Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+- Decision (superseded): a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Forbidden (superseded): renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
+- Reason (still the cost of the new decision): the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every
+  tab to the right of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29,
+  reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test, which was replaced by the renumbering tests on 2026-10-23).
